<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odessa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Odessa is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, founded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> in the 1790s. It grew rapidly as the main outlet for grain grown on the steppe of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Ukraine. Exports through Odessa underpinned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>'s position as a leading grain exporter.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odessa.jpg" alt="Odessa" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nata Naval · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%86%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%8F%D0%BA_17.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Odesa lies in the Black Sea Lowland, on the southwestern shore of the Gulf of Odesa on the northwestern coast of the Black Sea. It is about 31 km north of the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, some 443 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, and about 40 km east of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>. It is a warm-water port. The city is sometimes called the Pearl by the Sea or the Southern Capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A large Greek settlement stood on the site no later than the middle of the 6th century BCE, and a necropolis of the 5th to 3rd centuries BCE has long been known. Some scholars believe it was a trading settlement founded by the Greek city of Histria, though whether the bay is the ancient Port of the Histrians is not settled. A Slavic port, Kotsiubijiv, is first mentioned in 1415 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. After 1529 the area, known as Khadjibey, belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which rebuilt the fortress there in the mid-18th century under the name Yeni Dünya, meaning New World, until its defeat in the Russo-Turkish War ended in 1792.</p><p>A decree of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> in 1794 ordered a naval harbour and trading place at Khadjibey, and in 1795 it was officially renamed Odessa, after a Greek colony of Odessos that supposedly lay in the area. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, in 1797, counted 3,455 people, and by 1803 there were 9,000. Its first commercial bank opened in 1801. From 1819 to 1858 it was a free port.</p><p>In 1905 Odesa saw a workers' uprising supported by the crew of the battleship Potemkin, commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergei-eisenstein">Sergei Eisenstein</a>'s film The Battleship Potemkin, which includes a scene on the great stone staircase now known as the Potemkin Steps. In 1941 Romanian and German troops attacked the city, and the Siege of Odessa began on 5 August and lasted 73 days. In the 1991 referendum 85.38 per cent of Odesa Oblast voted for Ukrainian independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Odesa is the third most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and a major seaport and transport hub, with the Port of Odesa and the oil terminal at Port Pivdennyi. It is the administrative centre of Odesa Oblast. Its industries include shipbuilding, oil refining and chemicals. Its historic city centre, notable for its multiculturality and 19th-century urban planning, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odesa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 1890s Russia was among the world's leading grain exporters, shipping large quantities through the Black Sea ports of <b>Odessa</b> and Novorossiysk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Later periodicals included Bikurei ha-I'tim in Vienna (1820–1831), Kerem Hemed in Prague (1834–1857), Ha-Karmel in Vilnius (1860–1880), Ha-Tsefirah in Warsaw and Ha-Melitz in <b>Odessa</b>, launched in 1860.</blockquote><a href="/movement/haskalah">Haskalah · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The traditional account had him compose it in April 1898 in <b>Odessa</b>, while he was touring with his father's band.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-sole-mio">'O sole mio</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1918 Bolsheviks and their allies seized <b>Odessa</b> after street fighting with forces of the Ukrainian People's Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/odessa-soviet-republic">Odessa Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
